#!/bin/sh
#
# script/audits: when is the next audit due, and does the audit index still hold together?
#
# PROVISIONAL NAME. See the `Name:` block at the end of this comment.
#
# **The tripwire milestone 92 asks for, and the machinery milestone 93 shares.** An audit is a
# deliberate adversarial read of the tree through one named lens, and the failure mode this exists to
# prevent is not a bad audit: it is no audit, because auditing depended on someone remembering to
# ask. §74 decided what "due" means, on measured intervals rather than on a calendar somebody liked:
# event triggers first, a count second, six weeks as a backstop for a quiet period.
#
# **This never runs an audit and never edits anything.** Red means "run the audit", not "an
# automation ran it for you". That distinction is the whole reason the overdue check lives in a
# scheduled workflow (.github/workflows/audit-cadence.yml) rather than in script/lint: an audit
# coming due is not a defect in the commit that happened to trip the count, and a gate that blocked
# every unrelated merge behind a review nobody can hurry is the wall milestone 92 was written not to
# build. Same split, same reason, as toolchain-drift.yml.
#
# script/lint runs `--check`, which is the structural half and nothing else: the two tables in the
# index describe the same audits, every report link resolves, every kind has a cadence row, the
# dispositions parse. That one CAN fail a pull request, because a malformed index is a defect in the
# commit that malformed it.
#
# **Everything it knows comes from two places, and both are records a person maintains**: the index
# at design/audit-reports/README.md, and the tree itself. Nothing is stored in this script. The
# cadence numbers are in the index too rather than here, which is what lets milestone 93 add a
# documentation sweep by adding a row instead of by editing code, and what keeps the record and the
# signal from drifting apart the way script/roadmap keeps status honest by reading the milestone
# files.
#
#   script/audits             the report: every kind, its last audit, and every trigger's distance
#   script/audits --due       the tripwire: exit 1 if any kind is overdue, and say which trigger
#   script/audits --check     structural validation of the index; non-zero on a problem (script/lint)
#   script/audits --baseline  today's counts, to paste into a new baseline row
#   script/audits --worklist  which documents a documentation sweep should read first (milestone 93)
#
# **What the four counted triggers actually count**, because a number nobody can re-derive is a
# claim rather than evidence:
#
#   milestones built     BUILT rows in design/roadmap/README.md
#   components           crates/*/ plus [[bin]] targets in user/Cargo.toml
#   ABI constants        the `pub const NAME: u64` surface of crates/abi (see BUGS)
#   external packages    distinct registry packages across every committed Cargo.lock but vendor's
#
# The middle two are milestone 92's event triggers made countable. A new syscall method arrives as a
# new ABI constant; a new dependency (§46) arrives as a new external package. Both cadence rows are
# set to 1, so any change at all fires, which is what "event triggers are primary" means when a
# script has to evaluate it.
#
# BUGS.
#
# - **Two of milestone 92's four event triggers cannot be counted and are printed as a question.**
#   "A new component holding device or network authority" is a judgment about what a component does,
#   and "first boot on a new machine class" is about hardware that may not be in the tree at all.
#   This prints them on every run rather than filing them somewhere, which is rung four of CLAUDE.md's
#   ladder and is known to be the weakest part of the mechanism. The count triggers reach the same
#   place later, and later is the wrong word for an attack surface.
# - **The ABI-constant count is a superset of "a new syscall method".** It counts the rights bits,
#   the object types and the sentinels alongside the methods, so a new error sentinel fires the
#   trigger too. That is the tolerable direction for an audit signal, and separating them would mean
#   teaching this script the shape of every module in crates/abi, which is a copy of a fact that
#   already lives there.
# - **A change that lands as neither a milestone nor a component moves no number here.** A
#   substantial rewrite inside an existing crate is invisible to every count; only the calendar
#   backstop eventually catches it.
# - **It validates the baseline cells as integers, never as true.** Nothing re-derives them at the
#   commit they claim, so a mistyped baseline silently moves a trigger.
# - **It cannot tell a report that says nothing from one that found nothing.** `fixed 0, minted 0,
#   accepted 0` parses and passes.
# - **`--worklist` ranks documents it can parse a citation out of, and says nothing about the rest.**
#   138 of the 269 documents in its scope cite no code path it can resolve, and they are simply
#   absent from the list rather than reported as clean. It also never reads a sentence: a document at
#   the top may be perfectly true and one that is absent may be a year out of date. See
#   notes/documentation-audit.md, which is where the sweep procedure and this heuristic's limits are
#   written down.
# - **Adding a kind takes a cadence row and, if it has uncountable triggers, a `JUDGMENT` entry.**
#   The count triggers are index data on purpose; the judgment questions are prose that no table cell
#   holds, so they are in this script. That is one thing to remember, and it is rung four.
#
# **`minted` rather than `milestone` in the disposition vocabulary, and the reason is a collision
# with another gate rather than taste.** script/roadmap validates the phrase "milestone <number>"
# anywhere in the tree as a citation, so a findings cell with a count of zero parsed as a citation to
# milestone zero and failed the build. Caught on this milestone's first full lint. `minted` is also
# the verb this tree already uses for what the integrator does with a number, so the vocabulary got
# closer to the tree's own while it was being moved out of the way.
#
# Name: unrecorded. Provisional, minted by milestone 92's lane on 2026-08-16 and not yet put to
# calef, who names things. Refused `audit` (singular reads as a command that performs one, which is
# exactly the "an automation ran it for you" misreading the mechanism must not invite), `tripwire`
# (names the mechanism rather than the record, where this tree's family of report scripts is named
# for what it reads: `roadmap`, `decisions`, `names`), and `audit-cadence` (names the policy, while
# this also validates the index, which is not cadence). `audits` over `audit-reports` because the
# script answers about the audits and the directory holds the reports.
#
# See notes/scripts.md, design/audit-reports/README.md, and design/decisions/74-audit-cadence.md.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "${1:-}" <<'PY'
import datetime
import glob
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

root, mode = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
index_path = os.path.join(root, "design", "audit-reports", "README.md")

if mode not in ("", "--check", "--due", "--baseline", "--worklist"):
    print(f"audits: unknown argument {mode!r} "
          f"(no argument, --check, --due, --baseline, or --worklist)", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)

# The trigger names, in the order they are printed and in the order the cadence table's columns
# stand. `weeks` is last because it is the backstop rather than a peer of the other four.
COUNTS = ["built", "components", "abi", "packages"]
LABEL = {
    "built": "milestones built",
    "components": "components",
    "abi": "ABI constants",
    "packages": "external packages",
}

# The triggers no script can count, printed per kind under the kind they belong to. They are code
# rather than index data on purpose, and it is worth knowing why given that the cadence numbers went
# the other way: a number fits a table cell, and these are questions. A row shape that held one would
# hold one question per kind and no more, which is already wrong for `security` (it has two), and a
# free-form list in the index is a format for `--check` to police for no gain. The cost is that
# adding a kind means editing this dict as well as adding a row; the count triggers, which are the
# ones that actually fire, still need only the row. Recorded in the BUGS above.
JUDGMENT = {
    "security": [
        "has a new component taken device or network authority since the last audit?",
        "has this booted on a new machine class (a board, a cloud) since the last audit?",
    ],
    "documentation": [
        "has a subsystem been rewritten inside its existing crate since the last sweep? That "
        "moves no count here, and the note describing it from the outside is where it shows up.",
        "has a decision superseded a plan that a note still prescribes? notes/cpu-models.md's "
        "closing line was the 2026-08-03 exhibit.",
    ],
}

CADENCE_HDR = ("| Kind | Milestones | Components | ABI constants | External packages | Weeks |")
INDEX_HDR = "| Date | Kind | Lens | Findings | Report |"
BASE_HDR = ("| Date | Kind | Milestones built | Components | ABI constants "
            "| External packages |")

problems = []


# ---- what the tree looks like right now ---------------------------------------
# Every one of these is counted from the thing it describes, never remembered. The index carries the
# same four numbers as of each audit, taken the same way, and the deltas are the triggers.

def current():
    roadmap = os.path.join(root, "design", "roadmap", "README.md")
    with open(roadmap, errors="replace") as f:
        built = len(re.findall(r"^\| \d+ \| BUILT \|", f.read(), re.M))

    crates = len([d for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, "crates", "*")) if os.path.isdir(d)])
    with open(os.path.join(root, "user", "Cargo.toml"), errors="replace") as f:
        programs = f.read().count("[[bin]]")

    with open(os.path.join(root, "crates", "abi", "src", "lib.rs"), errors="replace") as f:
        abi = len(re.findall(r"^\s*pub const [A-Z_0-9]+: u64 = ", f.read(), re.M))

    # Discovered, not listed. A hard-coded set of lockfile paths is a copy of a fact about the
    # workspace layout, and script/drift's header records what that costs when it drifts. vendor/ is
    # somebody else's graph and enters our builds only through the consumers that depend on it.
    locks = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "*Cargo.lock"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.split()
    names = set()
    for lock in locks:
        if lock.startswith("vendor/"):
            continue
        with open(os.path.join(root, lock), errors="replace") as f:
            for block in f.read().split("[[package]]"):
                m = re.search(r'^name = "([^"]+)"', block, re.M)
                if m and 'source = "registry' in block:
                    names.add(m.group(1))

    return {"built": built, "components": crates + programs, "abi": abi, "packages": len(names)}


# ---- the index ----------------------------------------------------------------

def table(lines, header, path):
    """The rows under a header line, as lists of stripped cells."""
    try:
        start = lines.index(header)
    except ValueError:
        problems.append(f"{path}: no table headed {header!r}; has the index format changed?")
        return []
    rows = []
    for line in lines[start + 2:]:          # +2 skips the |---|---| separator
        if not line.startswith("|"):
            break
        rows.append([c.strip() for c in line.strip().strip("|").split("|")])
    return rows


try:
    with open(index_path, errors="replace") as f:
        lines = f.read().split("\n")
except FileNotFoundError:
    print(f"audits: {index_path} not found", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

DATE = re.compile(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
FINDINGS = re.compile(r"fixed (\d+), minted (\d+), accepted (\d+)$")
LINK = re.compile(r"\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\)$")

cadence = {}
for cells in table(lines, CADENCE_HDR, index_path):
    if len(cells) != 6:
        problems.append(f"cadence row {cells[0]!r}: {len(cells)} cells, expected 6")
        continue
    kind, nums = cells[0], cells[1:]
    if kind in cadence:
        problems.append(f"cadence: kind {kind!r} has two rows")
    bad = [n for n in nums if not (n.isdigit() and int(n) > 0)]
    if bad:
        problems.append(f"cadence row {kind!r}: {bad} are not positive integers; a trigger of 0 "
                        f"would fire forever and one that is not a number cannot fire at all")
        continue
    cadence[kind] = dict(zip(COUNTS + ["weeks"], (int(n) for n in nums)))

audits = []
for cells in table(lines, INDEX_HDR, index_path):
    if len(cells) != 5:
        problems.append(f"audit row {cells[0]!r}: {len(cells)} cells, expected 5")
        continue
    date, kind, lens, findings, report = cells
    if not DATE.match(date):
        problems.append(f"audit row {date!r}: not a YYYY-MM-DD date")
        continue
    fm = FINDINGS.match(findings)
    if not fm:
        problems.append(f"audit {date} ({kind}): findings {findings!r} must read "
                        f"'fixed N, minted N, accepted N'. Every finding ends in exactly one of "
                        f"the three states; \"noted\" is not a state (DECISIONS §35).")
    lm = LINK.match(report)
    if not lm:
        problems.append(f"audit {date} ({kind}): report {report!r} must be a markdown link")
    elif not os.path.exists(os.path.normpath(
            os.path.join(os.path.dirname(index_path), lm.group(2)))):
        problems.append(f"audit {date} ({kind}): report link {lm.group(2)!r} does not resolve")
    if not lens:
        problems.append(f"audit {date} ({kind}): no lens. The value of an audit is the lens the "
                        f"last one lacked, so a row without one cannot be read for what to do next.")
    audits.append({"date": date, "kind": kind, "lens": lens,
                   "findings": tuple(int(g) for g in fm.groups()) if fm else (0, 0, 0)})

baselines = {}
base_order = []
for cells in table(lines, BASE_HDR, index_path):
    if len(cells) != 6:
        problems.append(f"baseline row {cells[0]!r}: {len(cells)} cells, expected 6")
        continue
    date, kind, nums = cells[0], cells[1], cells[2:]
    if not DATE.match(date):
        problems.append(f"baseline row {date!r}: not a YYYY-MM-DD date")
        continue
    bad = [n for n in nums if n != "-" and not n.isdigit()]
    if bad:
        problems.append(f"baseline {date} ({kind}): {bad} are neither integers nor '-'")
        continue
    base_order.append((date, kind))
    baselines[(date, kind)] = {k: (None if n == "-" else int(n)) for k, n in zip(COUNTS, nums)}

# The two tables are one record split for two readers, so the check that keeps them one record is
# that they list exactly the same audits, in the same order. Without this, a lane can add a row to
# the table a person reads and leave the tripwire reading a baseline from the audit before it, which
# is the silent half-update that every two-file record eventually suffers.
index_order = [(a["date"], a["kind"]) for a in audits]
if index_order != base_order:
    only_index = [x for x in index_order if x not in base_order]
    only_base = [x for x in base_order if x not in index_order]
    if only_index or only_base:
        for d, k in only_index:
            problems.append(f"audit {d} ({k}) has no baseline row: the tripwire would read the "
                            f"previous audit's counts and under-report every trigger")
        for d, k in only_base:
            problems.append(f"baseline {d} ({k}) has no audit row")
    else:
        problems.append("the two tables list the same audits in different orders; keep both "
                        "oldest-first so the newest row is the last one in each")

if len(set(index_order)) != len(index_order):
    problems.append("two audits share a date and a kind; one of them cannot be found")
if index_order != sorted(index_order):
    problems.append("the audits are not in date order, and the newest row is what the tripwire "
                    "reads")
for kind in {a["kind"] for a in audits}:
    if kind not in cadence:
        problems.append(f"audit kind {kind!r} has no cadence row, so nothing can say when the next "
                        f"one is due. Add it to the cadence table (milestone 93 adds "
                        f"'documentation' this way).")

if mode == "--check":
    for p in sorted(set(problems)):
        print(f"audits: {p}", file=sys.stderr)
    if problems:
        sys.exit(1)
    kinds = ", ".join(sorted(cadence))
    print(f"audits: {len(audits)} on record, {len(cadence)} cadence ({kinds}), "
          f"both tables agree, every report resolves, every finding dispositioned")
    sys.exit(0)

if problems:
    # Anything below reads these tables, so a malformed one makes the answer a guess.
    for p in sorted(set(problems)):
        print(f"audits: {p}", file=sys.stderr)
    print("audits: the index does not parse, so nothing here can be computed "
          "(script/audits --check says the same thing and is what script/lint runs)",
          file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

now = current()

if mode == "--baseline":
    today = datetime.date.today().isoformat()
    print("Paste into design/audit-reports/README.md, both tables, oldest first:")
    print()
    print(f"| {today} | <kind> | <the lens this audit took> | fixed N, minted N, accepted N | "
          f"[<title>](../../notes/<report>.md) |")
    print(f"| {today} | <kind> | {now['built']} | {now['components']} | {now['abi']} "
          f"| {now['packages']} |")
    print()
    for k in COUNTS:
        print(f"  {LABEL[k]:<20} {now[k]}")
    sys.exit(0)


# ---- the worklist: which documents a documentation sweep should read first ------
#
# Milestone 93's block asks for this and leaves the shape open: "a note whose cited code has changed
# substantially since the note's last edit is the highest-value candidate for the next sweep, and git
# can compute that; whether it becomes a signal or stays a sweep heuristic is a decision for whoever
# builds this." It is a **heuristic, not a signal**: it never fails and nothing consumes its exit
# status. A document at the top of this list is a good guess about where claim rot is, and a
# confident guess about where it is not would be a lie, because the ranking cannot read a sentence.
#
# The rank is **how many of a document's cited files moved**, and the commit count only breaks ties.
# Ranking by commits alone puts every document that mentions xtask/src/main.rs on top, because that
# file and kernel/src/user/tests.rs are where every lane wires its test (CLAUDE.md names them as the
# merge hotspot). A note where eight of its twelve cited files moved has more surface that could have
# gone stale than one where a single hotspot moved thirty times.

def worklist():
    tracked = set(subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "ls-files"],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.split("\n"))
    tracked.discard("")

    # One walk of the whole history rather than two git calls per document. At 1,900 commits and 270
    # documents the difference is a second against a minute, and a heuristic nobody waits for is a
    # heuristic people run.
    log = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "log", "--format=%x00%cI", "--name-only"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
    touched, when = {}, None
    for line in log.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith("\x00"):
            when = line[1:]
        elif line.strip():
            touched.setdefault(line.strip(), []).append(when)

    # A backticked path, or a markdown link target. Bare basenames (`sched.rs`) are deliberately not
    # resolved: a basename is ambiguous across the tree, and every note in the sample that wrote one
    # also wrote the full path somewhere near it. See BUGS in notes/documentation-audit.md.
    cite = re.compile(r"`([A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]*\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)`|\]\(([^)#\s]+)\)")

    rows = []
    for doc in sorted(tracked):
        if not doc.endswith(".md"):
            continue
        # notes/ and design/roadmap/ and the repository-root documents. design/decisions/ is out:
        # a decision is a dated record of an argument, and code moving under it does not make it
        # wrong, it makes it history. Superseding a decision is a new decision, not an edit.
        if not (doc.startswith("notes/") or doc.startswith("design/roadmap/") or "/" not in doc):
            continue
        # CLAUDE.md is a symlink to AGENTS.md (they are one document with two names, for the
        # cross-tool convention), and listing the same prose twice costs a slot on the list.
        if os.path.islink(os.path.join(root, doc)):
            continue
        with open(os.path.join(root, doc), errors="replace") as f:
            text = f.read()
        cited = set()
        for m in cite.finditer(text):
            raw = m.group(1) or m.group(2)
            for cand in (raw, os.path.normpath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(doc), raw))):
                if cand in tracked and not cand.endswith(".md"):
                    cited.add(cand)
        edited = touched.get(doc, [None])[0]
        if edited is None:
            continue
        moved = {}
        for path in cited:
            n = sum(1 for t in touched.get(path, []) if t > edited)
            if n:
                moved[path] = n
        if moved:
            rows.append((len(moved), sum(moved.values()), doc, edited[:10], len(cited),
                         sorted(moved.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])))
    rows.sort(reverse=True)
    return rows


if mode == "--worklist":
    rows = worklist()
    print("Documents whose cited code has moved since they were last edited, most-moved first.")
    print("A heuristic for picking a documentation sweep's scope, never a verdict on a document:")
    print("nothing here has read a sentence. See notes/documentation-audit.md.")
    print()
    print(f"{'moved/cited':>12}  {'commits':>7}  {'last edit':<11} document")
    for n_moved, n_commits, doc, edited, n_cited, moved in rows[:30]:
        print(f"{n_moved:>5}/{n_cited:<6}  {n_commits:>7}  {edited:<11} {doc}")
        print(f"{'':>23}{', '.join(p for p, _ in moved[:3])}")
    print()
    print(f"{len(rows)} documents cite code that has moved under them.")
    sys.exit(0)


# ---- due, and why -------------------------------------------------------------

today = datetime.date.today()
due_kinds = []
out = []

for kind in sorted(cadence):
    rows = [a for a in audits if a["kind"] == kind]
    trigger = cadence[kind]
    if not rows:
        due_kinds.append(kind)
        out.append(f"{kind:<12}  never audited")
        out.append("  DUE: no audit of this kind has ever run, and its cadence row says one is "
                   "expected")
        for q in JUDGMENT.get(kind, []):
            out.append(f"  ? {q}")
        out.append("")
        continue

    last = rows[-1]
    base = baselines[(last["date"], last["kind"])]
    when = datetime.date.fromisoformat(last["date"])
    days = (today - when).days
    fired = []

    out.append(f"{kind:<12}  last {last['date']} ({last['lens'][:60]})")
    for k in COUNTS:
        want = trigger[k]
        if base[k] is None:
            out.append(f"  {LABEL[k]:<20} baseline not recorded, so this trigger cannot be "
                       f"evaluated")
            continue
        delta = now[k] - base[k]
        how = "any change fires" if want == 1 else f"fires at {want}"
        mark = "   <-- FIRED" if delta >= want else ""
        out.append(f"  {LABEL[k]:<20} {base[k]} -> {now[k]}  (+{delta}, {how}){mark}")
        if delta >= want:
            fired.append(f"{LABEL[k]} +{delta} (fires at {want})")
    weeks_days = trigger["weeks"] * 7
    mark = "  <-- FIRED" if days >= weeks_days else ""
    out.append(f"  {'calendar':<20} {days} day{'' if days == 1 else 's'} since "
               f"({trigger['weeks']} weeks){mark}")
    if days >= weeks_days:
        fired.append(f"calendar {days} days (fires at {weeks_days})")

    if fired:
        due_kinds.append(kind)
        out.append(f"  DUE: {'; '.join(fired)}")
    else:
        out.append("  not due")

    # Printed every run, due or not, and under the kind rather than at the end, because a reader who
    # has just been told "not due" is exactly the reader who has to answer these. Nothing standing
    # behind them but somebody reading this, which is rung four and known to be the weakest part.
    for q in JUDGMENT.get(kind, []):
        out.append(f"  ? {q}")
    out.append("")

print("\n".join(out))
print("The `?` lines are triggers nothing here can count, because they are a judgment and not a")
print("number. If one is yes, that kind is due regardless of everything above (milestone 92, §74).")
print()

if due_kinds:
    print(f"audits: {len(audits)} on record, DUE: {', '.join(due_kinds)}")
    print("Red means run the audit. Nothing here ran one, and nothing here can.")
    print("design/audit-reports/README.md says how, and which lens the last one lacked.")
    sys.exit(1 if mode == "--due" else 0)

print(f"audits: {len(audits)} on record, {len(cadence)} kind"
      f"{'' if len(cadence) == 1 else 's'}, none due")
PY
